    V2 SHARED-BASE(4): REFUSAL = ('COLUMN proposal by S01 ON THE SAME BASE', 18).  At that prefix (18 events: 1 mint + 15 deliveries + 1 proposal) the actor S01 holds the single lineage and already has a LIVE proposal on it.
      the layer's own option list `prop_options_in_view(view, S01)` = [] — EMPTY, because d42b1 skips a base on which the actor already has a live proposal; live triples in view = [('S01', ('v', ('v', 'v0'), (0,), ('S00',), 'S00'), 1)]; the whole menu offered to S01 has kinds ['n', 'r'] and contains NO proposal at all.
      MECHANISM, stated WITH THE STEP THAT MAKES IT TRUE (round-1 MINOR 5; the first version wrote 'two live bases per actor, i.e. 2g independent lineages' and the 'i.e.' skipped a step).  (1) A k-proposer conflict needs k live proposals ON ONE BASE, and one actor may hold AT MOST ONE live proposal per base — that is what the empty option list above shows, and it gives TWO BASES PER ACTOR, not yet 2g lineages.  (2) THE MISSING STEP: two concurrent groups CANNOT SHARE A BASE.  `admissible` requires triples(view, comp) == ckey for a WHOLE component, `View.components()` groups live proposals by base, and on one base the payload-0/payload-1 conflict graph of two groups is CONNECTED — so one base admits exactly one arbitration per generation.  (3) Hence g row bases + g column bases, pairwise distinct because every (r, c) cell sits in one of each: 2g lineages.  That is what V1's mints-first bootstrap builds and why it cannot be replaced by a single mint-and-spread.
      WHAT V2 IS AND IS NOT (round-1 MINOR 4).  `shared_base(4)` mints one version, spreads it to fifteen actors, makes ONE actor propose twice on that base back to back, and stops: it is a 18-event DEMONSTRATION OF `prop_options_in_view`, not an alternative schedule that was driven and failed, and it never attempts an arbitration.  IT DOES NOT SHOW THAT ONE LINEAGE PER ACTOR REFUSES: V3 CONFLICT-GRID(4, R) IS a one-lineage-per-actor design and it does NOT refuse — it forces, tiles and reaches width 8.  The scoped claim, and the only one made here, is that a grid whose rows and columns conflict CONCURRENTLY cannot run on one shared base.
