  [PASS] K1b(ii) THE DELIVERY ECONOMICS OF THE k = 4 ROTATION, MEASURED.  The concurrent (DOUBLE-GRID) schedule pays 2g(g-1) = 24 deliveries ONCE, in the bootstrap, and ZERO in every round thereafter — because `View.holdings` gives the minted version to ALL FOUR proposers and each actor's two axes live on two distinct unsuperseded bases.  The rotating (CONFLICT-GRID) schedule pays g(g-1) = 12 deliveries EVERY round, forever.  So at k = 4 the rotation is not merely more expensive, it is asymptotically more expensive, and the two-base holding pattern is what buys the difference  (in-round deliveries: DOUBLE-GRID/INTERLEAVED = 0 at every R (True); CONFLICT-GRID > 0 at every R (True); bootstrap deliveries at g = 4 = 24 of 120 events)

[K1c THE CONFLICT BUDGET AT k = 4 — how much of a record CAN be arbitration?  (pin: 'the budget bound gives <= 1/5 for conflict groups at k = 4')]
  BOUND (D66's counting theorem of the layer, re-run here, not re-derived).  An
  arbitration's ckey is a set of k live proposal triples and a proposal is
  resolved by at most one arbitration in any record, so #proposals >= k * #arbs
  and the arbitration share of a record is at most 1/(k+1) with k the smallest
  proposer count in it.  Deliveries only lower it.  The step D66's round-1
  MINOR 3 supplied: two arbitrations consuming the same proposal triple share
  that proposer's register, `event_poset` makes them causally COMPARABLE, and
  the later one's View has the triple in `resolved` — so `admissible` refuses it.
  ROUND-1 MINOR 1, THE LABEL CORRECTED BEFORE THE NUMBER IS READ.  The first
  version called 1/5 'the budget bound SATURATED'.  It is not this record's
  applicable bound: the DOUBLE-GRIDs mint their 2g lineages with ONE-PROPOSER
  arbitrations, so k_min = 1 and the bound that applies to THIS record is 1/2.
  Nor is 1/5 'the conflict share': the CONFLICT arbitrations are 16 of the 24
  and their share is 2/15.  What 1/5 equals is the bound that would apply to a
  record ALL of whose arbitrations had four proposers — which this one is not.
  The three readings are printed as three columns and never merged again.
